                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I'll be honest, I don't normally play these types of sim games, but I do sort of see the appeal. I went into this game hoping for a nice, casual experience fixing up some houses and, for the first few hours, that was more or less what it was. It was kind of fun to clean and paint houses and gain various upgrades along the way.\n\nAfter those first houses, though, the focus of the game shifted. Instead of cleaning houses and painting walls, the game started to focus more on placing furniture... [i]LOTS[/i] of furniture... as in well over a dozen pieces crammed into every room. I can only imagine that when the developers made the game, they probably had some people take the time to intricately design a well-made and interesting room with lots of furniture and decorations and said, \"That looks good. Let's send it!\" without actually considering that we, the players, can't read their minds and understand where everything was supposed to go. So when the game tells me that a simple room is supposed to have a bed, a couch, three tables, five chairs, three shelves, two bookcases, a dozen accessories and wall decorations, my reaction isn't, \"Oh wow, all this furniture is so cool!\", but rather more like, \"Holy CRAP, I'm supposed to fit all this in just ONE room... and there are FIVE MORE ROOMS after this one?\" Such a huge laundry list has me less focused on making the room actually look good and more on how tedious it is to cram everything in there, then go on to the next room and do the same thing all over again.\n\nWhat makes this whole process even worse is actually buying all of that furniture. All of it is done by opening the in-game tablet and scanning through the furniture catalog for every single piece of furniture. The first problem with this is the fact that navigating the tablet is very inefficient and unless you know/memorize where EVERY single piece of furniture is within the catalog's menus and pages, you are going to have to scan through all of those pages searching for the furniture, yourself. The best way to get what you want, then, is to instead grab a keyboard and use the tablet's Search feature to type in each piece of furniture's name and quickly find it that way. It doesn't help that the catalog for some reason separates multiple pieces of furniture that are very similar in name and, by our own human intuition, should basically be the same thing. For example, the game might have one piece of furniture called a \"Wooden Chair\" and another piece of furniture called a \"Dark Wood Chair\". By our intuition, those could essentially be interpreted to be the same thing, but according to the game, they are not. Therefore if the game specifically tells you to place a \"Wooden Chair\", you better place that exact piece of furniture called \"Wooden Chair\" or else it doesn't count.\n\nSo what does all of this ultimately mean? It means that when you walk into a house of seven rooms, every single one of those rooms is going to hit you with a list of 20 pieces of furniture that are all magically supposed to fit assuming you are someone who likes to spend half an hour on 3D jigsaw puzzles... multiplied by the number of rooms in that house. It also means that for every single piece of furniture you put in that room, you're going to have to open the tablet, click the Search bar, and type the EXACT name of the furniture you want. Suddenly, what should be a relaxing game of cleaning and fixing houses becomes an intense typing game where you are constantly machine-gunning names of furniture on your keyboard to try to complete each room within an even remotely reasonable amount of time. Maybe some of the later houses calm down on the furniture placement, but I don't have the patience to find out. I think that if you're someone who has a LOT of free time, is willing to put up with a TON of tedium, and doesn't mind doing doing a LOT of typing, then okay, you might like the idea of jigsaw-puzzling intricately and aesthetically designed rooms together for hours on end. However, if you're someone who actually wants a game that feels rewarding and like it actually respects your time, then I don't recommend this game at all.",
